    ["1648",null,"Cochecho Marsh adjoining parcel — John Baker","Dover granted Baker ten acres next to the marsh dividends. This was separate from his six-acre share.",30],
    ["1648",null,"Cochecho Marsh remainder — Richard Waldron","Dover assigned the undivided remainder to Richard Waldron after Baker’s ten-acre award. The quantity is unstated.",30],
    ["1642-06-11",null,"Maud house life interest","Dover granted Daniel and Mary Maud their dwelling for life, conditional on Daniel continuing as pastor. No land dimensions given.","23"],
    ["1643-10-10",null,"Pomfrett’s river neck","Dover assigned William Pomfrett the marsh and neck from the mouths of the Cochecho and Newichwannock rivers to the first narrows.","25"],
    ["1645",null,"Bloody Point addition to Dover","Massachusetts General Court assigned the neck to Dover as far south as the line between Canney’s Creek and Hogsty Cove. Residents had contested an earlier division between Dover and Strawberry Bank.","27"],
    ["1648-10-22",null,"Waldron’s lower Cochecho timber privilege","Dover granted Richard Waldron 1,500 pine or oak trees for the mill at the lower falls. Three pence was payable for each tree.","29"],
    ["1649-09-02",null,"Bellemies Bank Falls mill privilege","Dover granted William Pomfrett, Thomas Layton and John Dam the mill site, with 1,500 trees in the upstream swamp and limited cutting elsewhere on the river outside appropriated lots. Three pence per tree applied. A 1651 deed identifies the original as 23 August 1649.","32–34"],
    ["1649",null,"Oyster River Falls mill privilege","Dover granted Valentine Hill and Thomas Beard the falls. Wadleigh gives 19 September and the regional mill register gives 19 November Old Style. The exact date awaits the manuscript.","32"],
    ["1650-07-14",null,"Second Cochecho Falls mill privilege","Dover granted Thomas Wiggin and Edward Starbuck the site and adjoining timber for a mill. The rent was ten pounds annually. The grant prohibited an upstream mill that interfered with theirs.","32–33"],
    ["1650-07-14",null,"Wiggin’s upland at the second Cochecho falls","Dover allotted Captain Thomas Wiggin 100 acres beside the mill. The meeting of 10 July repeated the accommodation without identifying a separate parcel.","33"],
    ["1650-07-14",null,"Starbuck’s upland at the second Cochecho falls","Dover allotted Edward Starbuck fifty acres adjoining the mill.","33"],
    ["1651-07-24",null,"Wiggin’s Cochecho farm grant","Dover granted Thomas Wiggin 500 acres of upland for a farm at the second falls. An earlier instrument allotted 100 acres near the mill.","34"],
    ["1652",null,"Quamphegan Falls confirmation","Dover confirmed Thomas Wiggin and Simon Bradstreet in their mill privilege, with timber on a strip about two miles by one mile, for ten pounds in rent. Kittery also claimed jurisdiction there.","36"],
    ["1652",null,"Oyster River timber tract east of Johnson’s Creek","Dover granted Valentine Hill timber and land half a mile east of Thomas Johnson’s Creek at ten pounds rent.","36"],
    ["1652",null,"Hilton’s Point ox pasture","Dover granted the inhabitants of Dover Neck the swamp below the town extending to Hilton’s Point as common pasture.","36"],
    ["1652",null,"Dover Neck calves’ pasture","Dover allotted the waste land west of the Neck between John Hall and Philip Lewis’s lot and the waterside to the inhabitants for an enclosed calves’ pasture.","36"],
    ["1652-12-15",null,"Cochecho second falls, north bank","Dover granted Richard Walderne (Waldron) mill and timber privileges upstream from Wiggin and Starbuck’s earlier grant.","37"],
    ["1657-04-24",null,"Puscassicke mill farm allotments and boundary settlement","Exeter and Dover agreed that Thomas Kemball, William Hilton and Robert Smart could each hold sixty acres on the northwest side of the river, exempt from Dover rates. The mill tract included the neck below the lower falls, except six poles along the river. Exeter retained common grazing and cartways. Exeter marsh holdings were excepted from the Godard neck awarded to Dover.","41"],
    ["1657-05-25",null,"Rawson’s divided Cochecho farm survey","Bryan Pendleton and Peter Coffin, acting for the court, laid out 200 acres for Edward Rawson, half on each bank of the Cochecho just below the Indigenous path about three miles above Coffin’s house.","41"],
    ["1658-05-06",null,"Turner and Hilton’s pine-knot privilege","Dover selectmen granted Thomas Turner and William Hilton seventy loads from common land on the neck between Oyster and Back rivers. Existing private grants were excepted. The grantees owed Thomas Canney three pounds ten shillings.","43–44"],
    ["1662-02-13",null,"Barfoott’s Sandy Point flats","Dover granted Walter Barfoott eighty feet of tidal flats and twenty-four feet of upland, subject to earlier grants. Building within one year was a condition.","53"],
    ["1662-02-13",null,"Waldron’s Sandy Point enlargement","Dover granted Richard Waldron twenty-four feet of adjoining upland to enlarge his former tidal-flat grant.","53"],
    ["1662-04-25","1662-10-10","Coffin’s Sandy Point renewal","Dover renewed Peter Coffin’s grant through 29 September 1662 Old Style.","53"],
    ["1669-05-13",null,"Ileland Falls mill and land grant","Dover granted Robert Wadleigh a Lamprey River mill site with 100 acres on the south and twenty on the north, and timber within a mile of the river to the town boundary. Prior grants prevailed. Hatevil Nutter entered his dissent based on an earlier interest. Exeter issued its own overlapping authorization.","72–73"],
    ["1672-04-26",null,"Dover Neck ministry swamp","Dover set aside twenty acres in the great swamp on the Neck for the ministry. Alienation required the consent of all inhabitants.","76"],
    ["1673-02-04","1680-02-04","Watering-place path, Dover Neck","Dover selectmen granted John Roberts and Jeremie Tebbets the use of six common acres east of the path near Thomas Perkins’s house for seven years, after which the ground reverted to town pasture.","76"]
