  grant(nh,'1630-03-22',null,'Hilton’s Point patent (Wecanacohunt, Dover Neck)',council,'Edward Hilton, his heirs and assigns',
    '12 March 1629/30. The instrument describes the plantation of Hilton and his associates, the south bank to the falls, and a three-mile inland reach. Rival readings of the river calls later affected title.',
    'Jenness, Notes on the First Planting of New Hampshire, appendix I, Hilton’s Point patent.',piscataqua);
  grant(nh,'1631-11-13',null,'Pascataway patent (Piscataqua, Pascataquack)',council,'Ferdinando Gorges, John Mason, John Cotton, Henry Gardner, George Griffith, Edwin Guy, Thomas Wannerton, Thomas Eyre and Eliezer Eyre',
    '3 November 1631. The grant included Walter Neale’s house, cultivated ground and salt works. Calls run from a coastal point five miles west of the house past Hilton’s plantation and through the bay to the falls. It includes the Isles of Shoals and a separate strip on the opposite bank.',
    'Jenness, Notes on the First Planting of New Hampshire, appendix, pp. 82–84.',piscataqua);
  grant(nh,'1635-04-28','1635-06-21','Wollaston lease of New Hampshire and Masonia',council,'John Wollaston of London',
    '18 April 1635. The 3,000-year lease covered the Naumkeck–Piscataqua coast, southern Shoals islands and 10,000 acres southeast of the Sagadahoc mouth in Maine. Previously named island grants were excepted. The text gives inconsistent six- and sixty-mile inland calls. Wollaston assigned the residue of the lease to Mason on 11 June.',
    'Thorpe, Grant of the Province of New Hampshire to John Wollaston, 1635.',avalon+'nh02.asp');
  grant(nh,'1635-05-02',null,'Mason’s renewed New Hampshire grant and Masonia',council,'Captain John Mason and his heirs',
    '22 April 1635. Separate from Wollaston’s lease, this hereditary grant included Cape Ann, the coast from Naumkeck to Piscataqua, southern Shoals islands, and Masonia’s 10,000 acres southeast of the Sagadahoc entrance. It reserved appeals to the Council, excepted previously named island grants and reserved a share of precious-metal ore.',
    'New Hampshire State Papers XXIX, pp. 64–66.',avalon+'nh04.asp');
  grant(nh,'1635-06-21',null,'Assignment of Wollaston’s New Hampshire and Masonia lease','John Wollaston','Captain John Mason',
    '11 June 1635. Wollaston assigned the remaining term of his April lease, subject to its rent. It recites both the New Hampshire coast and the separate 10,000-acre Sagadahoc tract in Maine.',
    'New Hampshire State Papers XXIX, pp. 66–69.',avalon+'nh03.asp');
  grant(me,'1635-09-27',null,'Newichewanock river strip (Newichawannock)','Sir Ferdinando Gorges','Captain John Mason and his heirs',
    '17 September 1635. This subdivision of Gorges’s claim ran from the river entrance to its head, three miles inland and halfway across the river. The Crown retained a fifth of gold and silver ore.',
    'Thorpe, Grant of His Interest in New Hampshire by Sir Ferdinando Gorges to Captain John Mason.',avalon+'charter_008.asp');
  grant(nh,'1637-11-12',null,'Conditional Newbury grant at Winnacunnet or the lower Merrimack','Massachusetts General Court','the inhabitants of Newbury',
    '2 November 1637. The order offered a tract six miles square at Winnacunnet or another site below the first falls. Settlers moving within a year would receive three years’ immunity. The order leaves the site open. The 1638 grant named different petitioners.',
    'Lane Memorial Library, Founding Documents, General Court order of 2 November 1637.',hampton);
  grant(me,'1643-04-27',null,'Wheelwright’s individual Wells tract','Deputy Governor Thomas Gorges','John Wheelwright, pastor at Exeter',
    '17 April 1643. A separate Wells holding preceded Wheelwright’s appointment with Boade and Rishworth as plantation commissioners. The full Wells abstract describes 280 acres of upland including a neck northeast of Ogunquit River, and calls for 120 acres of adjoining marsh in the body despite a conflicting heading. Rent was five shillings annually.',
    'York Deeds I, part I, 28, excerpt in Publications of the Colonial Society of Massachusetts.',york);
  grant(me,'1643-07-24',null,'Wells Plantation township','Deputy Governor Thomas Gorges for Sir Ferdinando Gorges','John Wheelwright, Henry Boade and Edward Rishworth as commissioners of Wells',
    '14 July 1643. The town’s deed study identifies the three as commissioners. The bounds ran eight miles inland between the Ogunquit and Kennebunk rivers. The proprietor reserved decisions in disputes concerning admissions or allotments. A court memorandum confirmed the grant on 14 August 1644 O.S.',
    'Wells clerk records 1, pp. 1–2, and 1, p. 20, cited in Wells Coastal Beach Land Deed Research, findings, p. 4.',wells);
  grant(me,'1643-07-24',null,'Saunders neck between Little River and Cape Porpoise River','Deputy Governor Thomas Gorges','John Saunders of Wells',
    '14 July 1643. An individual grant and confirmation of 150 acres between the two rivers plus 50 acres of adjacent marsh, distinct from the plantation grant on the same day.',
    'York Deeds I, part II, 12, excerpt in Publications of the Colonial Society of Massachusetts.',york);

